style.text:
Let $\mathcal{P}$ be the set of all integers excluding zero. Determine all maps $g: \mathcal{P} \to [0, \infty) \cap \mathbb{Z}$ such that for any $x, y \in \mathcal{P}$:
(i) If $x+y \neq 0$, then $g(x+y) \geq \min(g(x), g(y))$;
(ii) $g(xy) = g(x) + g(y)$.

exact.text:
Let $S = \{ (x, y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : y^2 - x = \sqrt{x} \}$. 
\begin{itemize} 
\item[i)] Determine all elements of $S \cap (\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{Q})$. 
\item[ii)] Demonstrate that the set $S \cap \mathbb{Q}^2$ contains infinitely many points. 
\end{itemize}

