\begin{align*}\nx^2 - 9k &= 16 \\\ny^2 - 16k &= 9
\end{align*}

paraphrase.text:
Let $\omega = 5^{1/3} + 5^{2/3}$. Determine for each of the following elements $\eta \in \mathbb{Q}(5^{1/3})$ whether $\eta$ belongs to the subring $\mathbb{Z}[\omega]$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item $\eta = 220 \cdot 5^{2/3} + 284 \cdot 5^{1/3}$
    \item $\eta = 1184 \cdot 5^{2/3} + 1210 \cdot 5^{1/3}$
\end{enumerate}

Determine all $n \in \mathbb{Z}^+$ for which there exists a mapping $f: \{1, \dots, n\}^2 \to \{1, \dots, n\}^2$ defined by $f(i, j) \in \{(i-1, j), (i+1, j), (i, j-1), (i, j+1)\}$ such that:
(i) $f$ is a permutation of the set of cells;
(ii) for every $i \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}$, the number of cells $(i, j)$ such that $f(i, j) = (i-1, j)$ equals the number of cells $(i, j)$ such that $f(i, j) = (i+1, j)$;
(iii) for every $j \in \{2, \dots, n-1\}$, the number of cells $(i, j)$ such that $f(i, j) = (i, j-1)$ equals the number of cells $(i, j)$ such that $f(i, j) = (i, j+1)$.
